1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,672
This lesson follows the instructional themes of school 5B. Our
original scene and reflection tasks prepare the same skills without
reproducing the school competency check. Scene management is clinical
work: uncontrolled hazards can prevent care and create additional
patients.

2
00:00:18,672 --> 00:00:31,992
The sequence is not one safety check followed by a fixed checklist.
Risk changes from dispatch through approach, contact, care, movement
and transfer; reassessment belongs in every phase.

3
00:00:31,992 --> 00:00:42,696
We will use a fresh multi-vehicle scene to practise the school
frameworks. The case is original and does not reproduce the older
companion lesson or a school assessment answer.

4
00:00:42,696 --> 00:01:00,264
Operational risk assessment means noticing a hazard, considering who
could be harmed and deciding what is safe enough to do next. It is
repeated, not completed once at the curb. Infection-control point-of-
care risk assessment is a related but more specific exposure decision.

5
00:01:00,264 --> 00:01:13,104
A short verbal estimate can be practical: what can happen, how soon,
who is in the exposure path, and which reliable control would reduce
the risk. A numerical score never grants permission to enter.

6
00:01:13,104 --> 00:01:27,168
If there is an uncontrolled threat, the correct clinical move may be
to stop, increase distance, reposition, withdraw or wait for a
qualified specialist. That pause protects the ability to return and
provide care.

7
00:01:27,168 --> 00:01:44,280
Operational risk assessment covers traffic, unstable structures,
violence, machinery, weather and the work itself. In infection
control, point-of-care risk assessment more specifically considers
infectious exposure during a particular patient interaction.

8
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:56,976
These are connected questions, not interchangeable labels. A mask
decision does not clear a traffic hazard; moving to a safer road
position does not decide whether suctioning could expose your face.

9
00:01:56,976 --> 00:02:11,712
Prefer controls that reduce exposure before relying only on personal
protective equipment. A controlled access route, assigned traffic
management and a prepared clean work area can prevent hazards that
gloves cannot stop.

10
00:02:11,712 --> 00:02:23,616
Say the decision aloud: the hazard, who is exposed, the control and
what would make you stop. Revisit it when the patient, procedure,
environment or available protection changes.

11
00:02:23,616 --> 00:02:39,984
A 360-degree survey deliberately looks beyond the patient and the
first obvious mechanism. Observe approach and exit routes, overhead
loads, ground stability, power sources, smoke direction, weather and
where other people are moving.

12
00:02:39,984 --> 00:02:53,760
Different hazard families can combine. A vehicle collision may include
traffic, leaking fuel, damaged power lines, agitated bystanders and a
changing wind that carries vapour toward the staging area.

13
00:02:53,760 --> 00:03:03,600
The survey is not a guarantee that the scene is safe. It creates a
working picture that must be updated whenever the scene, task or
patient condition changes.

14
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:17,280
METHANE organizes an early report: Major incident declared, Exact
location, Type of incident, Hazards, Access, Number and type of
casualties, and Emergency services required or present.

15
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:29,640
A useful report is concise and updateable. State what you know, label
estimates as estimates, identify the main hazard and describe the
access or staging issue that changes responder safety.

16
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:39,768
Do not delay an initial warning while trying to complete every field
perfectly. Give the critical known facts, then update command as
information improves.

17
00:03:39,768 --> 00:03:57,528
Here is an original report: Control, Unit Seventy-Two, possible major
incident at Ridgeway depot west gate. A canopy has collapsed.
Stability and power status are not confirmed. Hold incoming crews at
the west access point.

18
00:03:57,528 --> 00:04:11,208
Continue: Seven patients are visible from our position; that count is
provisional. Request fire assistance and additional ambulances. We
will update the safe access route after scene control is confirmed.

19
00:04:11,208 --> 00:04:23,904
The report names uncertainty instead of disguising it as certainty. Do
not announce that the area is safe because you cannot see smoke, or
silently turn a visible count into a complete casualty count.

20
00:04:23,904 --> 00:04:36,936
A later update might change the count or close an access route.
Identify the change and obtain acknowledgment for a critical
restriction. The shared incident picture must change when the evidence
changes.

21
00:04:36,936 --> 00:04:47,952
Reassess after wind shifts, fire or traffic changes, a load moves, a
new patient appears, a bystander becomes threatening or protective
equipment is damaged.

22
00:04:47,952 --> 00:04:58,200
Before movement, verify that the route, people and equipment still
support a safe transfer. A safe approach does not prove that the
egress route is safe.

23
00:04:58,200 --> 00:05:10,848
If the risk picture becomes unacceptable, stop work and report the
reason. A useful stop-work message names the hazard, its location, who
is exposed and what support or control is needed.

24
00:05:10,848 --> 00:05:24,504
An incident management system creates a shared structure for command,
operations, planning, logistics and information. A small incident may
combine roles; a larger event separates them as needed.

25
00:05:24,504 --> 00:05:36,840
The system reduces duplicated work and conflicting instructions.
Personnel should know who supervises them, what task they own, where
they report and how urgent changes will be communicated.

26
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:56,544
Use the current BC incident structure and dispatch procedures. First-
arriving responsibilities include initial command and triage
functions; a later arrival or higher clinical rank does not
automatically transfer those roles. Establish, communicate and hand
over the assignment explicitly.

27
00:05:56,544 --> 00:06:10,464
The current BCEHS MCI page divides first-arriving responsibilities
into Incident Commander and Triage Leader functions. The initial
command function collects the scene picture and communicates with the
communication centre.

28
00:06:10,464 --> 00:06:23,616
This is not the same as inventing an independent command system.
Establish the appropriate initial organization under the service plan,
identify yourself clearly and request the resources the scene needs.

29
00:06:23,616 --> 00:06:36,912
The initial command and triage roles remain in place unless
appropriately relieved. A later arrival or higher clinical
qualification does not, by itself, tell everyone that command has
changed.

30
00:06:36,912 --> 00:06:51,696
Use the deliberate transfer already taught: situation, hazards,
objectives, resources, unresolved problems and acceptance. Tell
affected crews who now holds the role and which instructions have
changed.

31
00:06:51,696 --> 00:07:07,656
A triage lead organizes rapid sorting and reports the emerging patient
picture. A treatment lead organizes care after that first sort.
Confusing these jobs can leave everyone treating one patient while
other patients remain unseen.

32
00:07:07,656 --> 00:07:22,224
Transport coordination links patient priority, available crews and
receiving capacity. Staging keeps arriving units available and
accounted for instead of allowing each unit to choose its own patient
or destination.

33
00:07:22,224 --> 00:07:35,664
A scribe preserves important times, counts, assignments and updates.
Small incidents may combine functions, but the team still needs to
know who owns each decision and where information must go.

34
00:07:35,664 --> 00:07:49,176
Closed-loop tasking means the recipient repeats the critical
assignment and reports completion or a problem. Saying that someone
should arrange transport is weaker than assigning the task to a named
role and confirming it.

35
00:07:49,176 --> 00:08:01,848
A transfer of command is a deliberate handoff, not an assumption. The
outgoing lead gives the current situation, hazards, objectives,
resources, decisions and unresolved risks.

36
00:08:01,848 --> 00:08:12,504
The incoming lead confirms acceptance and states immediate priorities.
Crews need to know the new command point and whether assignments or
restricted areas have changed.

37
00:08:12,504 --> 00:08:24,912
Use plain language and read-back for critical instructions. When a
communication fails or a hazard changes, correct the shared picture
promptly rather than assuming everyone heard the first message.

38
00:08:24,912 --> 00:08:36,336
Routine infection-prevention measures begin with hand hygiene and a
point-of-care risk assessment. Add precautions according to the likely
transmission route and current local policy.

39
00:08:36,336 --> 00:08:48,816
PPE must match both the hazard and the task. Gloves do not replace eye
protection when splash is likely; a surgical mask does not protect
against an unknown airborne chemical atmosphere.

40
00:08:48,816 --> 00:09:01,584
Use the taught donning, removal, disposal and hand-hygiene sequence.
If equipment is damaged, contaminated or outside your training,
withdraw and follow the supervisor’s plan.

41
00:09:01,584 --> 00:09:18,792
The school groups standard, contact, droplet and airborne precautions
together. In Canadian terminology, routine practices apply to every
patient; contact, droplet and airborne precautions are additional
layers, sometimes used together.

42
00:09:18,792 --> 00:09:35,544
Routine practice does not mean gloves for every possible touch. Select
gloves for the expected task and exposure, including blood, body
fluids, mucous membranes, non-intact skin or contaminated items, and
follow local policy.

43
00:09:35,544 --> 00:09:51,576
The transmission concern changes which barriers and environmental
controls are needed. A coughing patient may need source control and
additional protection; one symptom does not let you invent a diagnosis
or ignore current pathogen-specific direction.

44
00:09:51,576 --> 00:10:04,608
Hand hygiene, equipment cleaning and clean-to-dirty task organization
still matter under every layer. More PPE cannot repair contaminated
technique or an uncontrolled chemical atmosphere.

45
00:10:04,608 --> 00:10:16,272
First choose the equipment for the task and check its condition. Plan
where it will be removed and discarded. Clean your hands before
beginning; gloves are not a replacement for that step.

46
00:10:16,272 --> 00:10:33,600
The PHAC Appendix Ten example then uses gown, mask or respirator, eye
protection and gloves. When a gown is required, the gloves cover its
cuffs. This is a named example, not a claim that every specialized
ensemble uses one order.

47
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:48,144
For a tight-fitting respirator, use the make, model and size selected
through fit testing and perform the user seal check as taught. A face
shield adds eye or splash protection; it does not replace respiratory
protection.

48
00:10:48,144 --> 00:11:01,680
Before approaching, check that the items work together and allow the
intended task. If a seal or barrier is not adequate, resolve the
problem before exposure rather than hoping the next layer will
compensate.

49
00:11:01,680 --> 00:11:16,200
PPE removal can transfer contamination onto hands, clothing or the
face. Prepare the waste or reprocessing route and remove items
deliberately, avoiding unnecessary contact with contaminated fronts.

50
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:31,776
The PHAC example removes gloves and gown, cleans hands, removes eye
protection and then the mask or respirator, and cleans hands again.
Handle straps, ties or the designated clean parts rather than the
exposed front.

51
00:11:31,776 --> 00:11:46,344
The saved school narrative orders some steps differently. Keep that
source difference visible and use the complete current employer or
station protocol for the actual ensemble and removal location. Do not
create a hybrid order from memory.

52
00:11:46,344 --> 00:12:01,992
If you contaminate your hands during removal, stop and perform the
indicated hand hygiene before continuing. Report a breach, manage the
exposed equipment appropriately and seek the required exposure follow-
up rather than silently carrying on.

53
00:12:01,992 --> 00:12:14,952
A fit test selects and evaluates a specific respirator make, model and
size on a person. A user seal check is performed when putting it on.
Passing one does not eliminate the need for the other.

54
00:12:14,952 --> 00:12:29,352
Facial hair in the sealing area, a changed face shape, incompatible
eyewear or a different model may interfere with protection. Report the
issue and use the respiratory-protection programme instead of
improvising a fit solution.

55
00:12:29,352 --> 00:12:40,320
The testing schedule comes from the applicable programme and
jurisdiction. Do not turn a generic educational interval into a claim
about the school or your employer’s current schedule.

56
00:12:40,320 --> 00:12:53,592
A filtering medical respirator does not solve an unknown chemical gas
exposure or a low-oxygen atmosphere. That is a different hazard
assessment requiring the incident’s specialist protection and access
plan.

57
00:12:53,592 --> 00:13:07,560
WHMIS/GHS labels and Safety Data Sheets help identify a known
product’s hazards, routes of exposure, storage and emergency
information. Learn how to locate them through an authorized source.

58
00:13:07,560 --> 00:13:17,616
Do not approach a leaking container simply to read its label. Use
information supplied by witnesses, shipping documents or incident
command from a safe position.

59
00:13:17,616 --> 00:13:32,496
Unknown concentration, oxygen deficiency or reactive mixtures cannot
be made safe by ordinary medical PPE. Isolation, monitoring and
decontamination are specialist functions under the incident plan.

60
00:13:32,496 --> 00:13:46,704
A flame and a flame over a circle are not interchangeable. The flame
identifies specified fire-related hazards; the flame over a circle
indicates an oxidizer, which can intensify combustion.

61
00:13:46,704 --> 00:14:01,056
A cylinder symbol warns of pressure-related hazards. An exploding-bomb
symbol identifies specified severe reactive hazards. Neither symbol
tells a medical crew that a damaged container is safe to touch or
move.

62
00:14:01,056 --> 00:14:13,152
Corrosion can concern skin, eyes or metals according to the product
classification. Do not assume that all corrosives have the same
concentration, first aid or protective equipment.

63
00:14:13,152 --> 00:14:24,144
The standard symbols shown here support visual recognition. The full
label and Safety Data Sheet determine the product information; the
incident plan determines what you may safely do.

64
00:14:24,144 --> 00:14:38,736
The skull and crossbones identifies specified severe acute toxicity.
The exclamation mark covers other specified harmful or irritant
effects. The difference does not mean that an exclamation-mark product
is harmless.

65
00:14:38,736 --> 00:14:54,288
The human silhouette with a chest starburst identifies specified
serious health hazards. Read the hazard statement to learn whether the
concern is respiratory sensitization, organ toxicity, reproductive
effects or another listed hazard.

66
00:14:54,288 --> 00:15:06,792
Canada’s biohazardous infectious materials symbol has a black circular
border, not the usual red diamond. The environment symbol may appear
but is not required by WHMIS.

67
00:15:06,792 --> 00:15:21,624
Some hazardous classes do not require a pictogram at all. No symbol, a
missing label or an unknown product is not evidence of safety; report
uncertainty and obtain the correct product information without
approaching the hazard.

68
00:15:21,624 --> 00:15:36,888
A supplier label connects the product identifier, supplier,
pictograms, signal word, hazard statements and precautionary
statements. Danger and Warning are signal words; they do not replace
the actual hazard description.

69
00:15:36,888 --> 00:15:49,224
Check that the Safety Data Sheet matches the product. In its sixteen-
section format, identification and hazards come first; the revision
date helps you recognize which document you are using.

70
00:15:49,224 --> 00:16:05,976
For an exposure question, find first aid in section four. Release
information is in section six, exposure controls in section eight and
reactivity in section ten. Use the relevant information with
specialist advice rather than guessing from a symbol.

71
00:16:05,976 --> 00:16:20,712
An SDS describes a product; it does not measure oxygen, concentration
or all mixtures at this incident. Reading it from a safe place can
improve the report but does not grant entry or permission to clean a
spill.

72
00:16:20,712 --> 00:16:34,752
In a mass-casualty incident, triage aims to sort patients rapidly so
limited resources are directed according to urgency. It is a time-
limited first pass inside incident command, not complete clinical
care.

73
00:16:34,752 --> 00:16:47,592
START is an adult-oriented framework; JumpSTART adapts the approach
for children. Use the exact version taught by the school and current
service plan rather than blending algorithms from different posters.

74
00:16:47,592 --> 00:16:57,912
Color categories communicate a priority, not a guarantee of outcome or
a permanent label. A patient can move to a different priority when new
findings emerge.

75
00:16:57,912 --> 00:17:12,096
The current cards use Minor in green, Delayed in yellow, Immediate in
red and Deceased in black. Match the actual local label and tracking
process. A color is useful only if the team shares its meaning.

76
00:17:12,096 --> 00:17:26,976
Minor does not mean no injury, and Delayed does not mean no care. They
describe lower priority relative to immediate threats during this
sort. Arrange the designated review and treatment process rather than
leaving those groups unattended.

77
00:17:26,976 --> 00:17:43,176
The pediatric chart sends walking children to Minor and then secondary
triage. For a child who cannot walk because of developmental stage or
disability, inability to walk alone is not a category; use the current
tool and appropriate secondary assessment.

78
00:17:43,176 --> 00:18:00,240
Deceased is the posted MCI tool’s label, not a new independent
authority to pronounce death, abandon a patient or apply this
algorithm to ordinary resuscitation. Categories, patient tracking and
reassessment operate within the activated incident plan.

79
00:18:00,240 --> 00:18:14,928
The current adult card first asks about walking on command and absence
of major injury. Do not make every walking person automatically safe.
For a non-walker, assess breathing and the specified airway-
positioning branch.

80
00:18:14,928 --> 00:18:31,344
If breathing remains absent after positioning, the card label is
Deceased; if breathing starts, it is Immediate. This is the activated
incident’s triage tool, not permission to replace ordinary
resuscitation decisions outside that context.

81
00:18:31,344 --> 00:18:49,896
With breathing present, use respiratory rate, radial pulse and simple-
command response. More than thirty breaths per minute, an absent
radial pulse or inability to follow a simple command reaches
Immediate. Exactly thirty does not cross the rate branch by itself.

82
00:18:49,896 --> 00:19:04,968
Other patients following that pathway are Delayed. Keep the approved
brief lifesaving actions within the triage role; do not turn the first
pass into full treatment or import an older capillary-refill threshold
into this radial-pulse card.

83
00:19:04,968 --> 00:19:19,320
In the current child chart, a non-walking child without spontaneous
breathing first receives airway positioning. If breathing begins, the
category is Immediate. Do not put the pulse check ahead of this airway
step.

84
00:19:19,320 --> 00:19:32,880
If apnea persists, assess a palpable pulse. No pulse in this branch
leads to the chart’s Deceased label. If a pulse is present, the chart
directs five rescue breaths using the taught technique.

85
00:19:32,880 --> 00:19:49,320
Reassess breathing after those breaths. If it resumes, select
Immediate; if apnea continues, follow the Deceased branch. The order
matters because a child with respiratory arrest can differ from an
adult in the corresponding rapid-sort tool.

86
00:19:49,320 --> 00:20:03,552
These are MCI classification branches under the current incident plan
and trained role. A label is not a stand-alone death pronouncement or
a replacement for routine pediatric resuscitation outside the MCI
process.

87
00:20:03,552 --> 00:20:19,368
For the breathing pathway, a rate below fifteen or above forty-five
per minute reaches Immediate. A rate of exactly fifteen or forty-five
stays within the range and continues to the next branch; it does not
by itself establish Delayed.

88
00:20:19,368 --> 00:20:30,192
A breathing child without a palpable pulse reaches Immediate in the
perfusion branch. Do not confuse that with the different apneic, no-
pulse branch we just followed.

89
00:20:30,192 --> 00:20:47,568
The current graphic uses AVPU: Alert, response to Voice, response to
Pain, or Unresponsive. Alert, voice response or an appropriate pain
response follows Delayed; an inappropriate pain response or
unresponsiveness follows Immediate.

90
00:20:47,568 --> 00:21:03,120
The graphic gives withdrawal as an example of an appropriate pain
response and posturing as an inappropriate one. Use the trained
assessment; do not substitute a full GCS or classify a normally non-
walking child by mobility alone.

91
00:21:03,120 --> 00:21:13,752
A quiet patient can deteriorate, and a patient initially classified as
delayed can become immediate. Reassess at the interval and triggers in
the current local plan.

92
00:21:13,752 --> 00:21:22,680
Use a clear tag and tracking process so that triage category, patient
movement, treatment and transport assignment remain connected.

93
00:21:22,680 --> 00:21:34,296
Secondary triage and clinical treatment occur in designated areas
under command. Do not allow a self-selected destination or ad hoc
transport to defeat the coordinated plan.

94
00:21:34,296 --> 00:21:48,936
In an original rehearsal at a cleared shelter walkway, Adult A cannot
walk but breathes twenty-eight times per minute, has a radial pulse
and follows a simple command. On the adult card, that pathway reaches
Delayed.

95
00:21:48,936 --> 00:22:02,400
Adult B breathes thirty-one times per minute. The rate already reaches
Immediate. A child breathing forty-six times per minute also reaches
Immediate, but through the pediatric rate boundary, not the adult
number.

96
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:16,944
Later Adult A stops following simple commands. Repeat the relevant
assessment, identify the new Immediate concern and update the tag and
treatment team through the incident process. The original label is not
permanent.

97
00:22:16,944 --> 00:22:30,096
A useful update preserves patient identity, the changed finding and
time, and the requested response. Do not remove the old timeline or
send an untracked patient toward a self-selected ambulance.

98
00:22:30,096 --> 00:22:46,896
CBRNE stands for Chemical, Biological, Radiological, Nuclear and
Explosive. A cluster of similar illness, unexplained odour, unusual
residue, damaged containers or a scene inconsistent with the call can
raise concern.

99
00:22:46,896 --> 00:22:58,368
These clues are not a field diagnosis. Avoid touching, smelling
closely, sampling or moving suspected material; relay observations
from a safe position.

100
00:22:58,368 --> 00:23:09,984
A secondary device or continuing source may threaten responders after
the first incident. Maintain distance, report the suspected hazard and
await specialist direction.

101
00:23:09,984 --> 00:23:21,168
Incident command and hazardous-material specialists define zones,
access routes, decontamination and monitoring. Labels alone do not
show that an area is safe.

102
00:23:21,168 --> 00:23:31,248
Current BCEHS CBRNE guidance places clinical care in the cold zone.
Medical awareness does not authorize routine entry into a hot or warm
zone.

103
00:23:31,248 --> 00:23:41,856
Do not enter to retrieve a patient, equipment or a partner without the
required command authorization, specialist PPE, training and rescue
capability.

104
00:23:41,856 --> 00:23:57,096
Current BCEHS PR05 requires CliniCall contact with a paramedic
specialist before assessing patients from a hazardous-material or
chemical-exposure incident. Ordinary BCEHS equipment does not
authorize work in the hot or warm zone.

105
00:23:57,096 --> 00:24:14,760
Under the controlled plan, safe evacuation and clothing removal reduce
ongoing exposure and secondary contamination. Explain the process,
preserve dignity, arrange assistance for those who cannot follow
instructions and control removed contaminated items.

106
00:24:14,760 --> 00:24:33,696
The school’s blanket no-soap statement differs from PR05, which
includes mild soap for wet decontamination. Do not infer that every
unknown agent should simply be flooded with water; the dry or wet
method is contaminant- and plan-dependent. Avoid abrasive scrubbing.

107
00:24:33,696 --> 00:24:53,304
The CBRNE guidance prioritizes lifesaving airway and hemorrhage care
over waiting for perfect decontamination, within safe roles and
protection. This does not remove zone boundaries. Coordinate care,
prevent hypothermia and notify receiving services about contamination
risk.

108
00:24:53,304 --> 00:25:07,488
A freight truck has overturned at a rural intersection. Several people
are injured, a cargo drum is damaged, and the contents are not yet
known. A responder sees vapour near the truck but has not entered the
area.

109
00:25:07,488 --> 00:25:20,856
Before anyone approaches, the wind shifts toward the initial staging
point. The team withdraws to the new safe side, warns crews already
moving in and reports the changed hazard and access route.

110
00:25:20,856 --> 00:25:35,136
Command requests specialist hazardous-material support, confirms zone
controls, and assigns a triage leader once a safe casualty area is
established. The medical crew does not cross into an uncontrolled
zone.

111
00:25:35,136 --> 00:25:46,776
A learning review asks what was expected, what actually happened, what
changed, and what the team should keep or improve. It is focused on
systems and decisions rather than blame.

112
00:25:46,776 --> 00:25:57,000
Protect patient and staff privacy in notes and discussion. Share only
what is needed for handover, safety learning or the authorized
reporting process.

113
00:25:57,000 --> 00:26:08,688
The module’s scenario and reflection sections need active practice. A
narrated lesson cannot demonstrate scene command, PPE competency or
safe triage performance for you.

114
00:26:08,688 --> 00:26:23,280
Experience helps only when you test whether the present scene is
actually similar to the last one. Emotional urgency, a familiar
address or a loud casualty can pull attention away from an unseen
hazard or quieter patient.

115
00:26:23,280 --> 00:26:39,240
In the review, name the cue you missed, the decision it affected and a
practical repair. For example, a change report was transmitted but not
acknowledged; the repair is a closed-loop update, not simply telling
everyone to be more careful.

116
00:26:39,240 --> 00:26:56,880
Check readiness after a difficult incident. If concentration,
emotional control or safe task performance is affected, tell the
appropriate lead and use the available peer, supervisor or
professional support pathway. You do not need to diagnose yourself to
ask for help.

117
00:26:56,880 --> 00:27:11,160
Keep operational learning specific and protect privacy. Restore
equipment and supplies, record authorized follow-up and make the
agreed change visible before the next call rather than treating the
debrief as the end of the work.
